  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 239 PM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThe upper high over the TX Panhandle will move into southern NM \non Thursday, while the aformentioned disturbance rotates around it\ninto Colorado. Showers and storms will focus over the northern \nmountains on Thursday, with much less coverage underneath the \nridge thanks to mid-level warming and drier air working its way \nin from the east. A few severe storms could develop with updrafts \nthat roll off the Sangre de Cristo Mountains where there will be \nample instability. The center of the ridge will then setup over \ncentral NM on Friday and Saturday, with moisture recycling around \nit. Moisture levels will be near seasonal normals, but flash \nflooding is a concern considering the slow and erratic storm \nmotions over the high terrain, including the Sacramento Mountains.\n500 mb heights between 596-598 dam should help to limit \ninstability somewhat, but hot afternoon temps and PWATs between \n1-1.25\" could counteract this.\n\nHeat will also become a concern late week into the weekend, \nespecially across the eastern plains where highs will reach into the \nlow 100s each day. Overnight lows will be above average as well (low \nto mid-70s) so there will not be significant relief from the heat at \nnight. High temps will be pushing 100F along the Rio Grande Valley \nas well, including in Albuquerque so heat risk concerns will also \nextend into central NM.",
